\section{Why nine of eleven passes partition}{

Seven passes block on \code{state} outright, and \code{A1-name-zip5} and \code{D2-prefix}
block on ZIP5, which determines state. A query can only reach a reference row
in its own state under any of them, so holding the other 89\% of the reference
buys nothing. The two that genuinely cross state lines — \code{C1-name-any} and
\code{C2-dba-any} — are also the cheapest in the cascade, 6,284 and 176 candidates
against \code{B2-token-concat}'s 71,105.
}

\section{The two things that make a slice behave like the whole}{

\strong{Corpus statistics.} \code{np_block()} gates tokens on document frequency and
IDF, both derived from the reference. Rebuilt on a slice they describe
California rather than the country, and a token common nationally but rare
in-state stops being gated — on the first attempt that produced \emph{more}
candidate pairs from a tenth of the reference. \code{\link[=pf_partition_reference]{pf_partition_reference()}}
therefore builds a \strong{hybrid} index: rows positional within the slice, because
\code{ref_index$base$row} indexes the frame it was built from, and \code{df_base},
\code{df_concat} and \code{n_reference} taken from the whole corpus.

\strong{Order of passes.} \code{np_cascade()} withholds queries that an earlier pass
resolved to YES, so a query's candidate set depends on how far it travelled.
Running the state-local passes first and the cross-state passes on what
remains changes what is pending when \code{C1}/\code{C2} run, relative to the monolithic
order where they sit between \code{B2} and \code{D1}. That is a real difference and it
is why \code{\link[=pf_run_partitioned]{pf_run_partitioned()}} is validated on tier agreement rather than
assumed identical.
